(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["coronary"]="<h1>Coronary Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-separate-blood-supply\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> Why does the heart need a separate blood supply?</h2>\n<p>The blood inside the chambers can't nourish the thick ventricular walls: oxygen can only diffuse a fraction of a millimetre. So the heart has its own arteries, the <strong>right and left coronary arteries</strong>. They arise from the <strong>aortic sinuses</strong>, the small pouches just above the aortic valve cusps.</p>\n<p><strong>The heart already takes out most of its oxygen at rest</strong> (about 70%, compared with about 25% for the body as a whole). So when it works harder, it can't simply extract more. <strong>Coronary blood flow has to rise.</strong> <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/co-measurement?section=7-why-does-pressure-loading-increase-the-heart-s-oxygen-demand\">→ Measuring Cardiac Output, §7</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Oxygen delivered = coronary blood flow × oxygen content of the blood.</strong> If either falls, the heart muscle runs short.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-trace-coronary-inflow-and-venous-drainage\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> Trace coronary inflow and venous drainage</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blausen_0260_CoronaryVessels_Anterior.png?width=800\" srcset=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blausen_0260_CoronaryVessels_Anterior.png?width=480 480w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blausen_0260_CoronaryVessels_Anterior.png?width=800 800w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blausen_0260_CoronaryVessels_Anterior.png?width=1200 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 700px) 92vw, 760px\" alt=\"Anatomical anterior view of the human heart showing the left and right ventricles and coronary arteries running over their surfaces\"><figcaption><strong>Anterior heart and ventricular coronary vessels.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Blausen Medical Communications, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blausen_0260_CoronaryVessels_Anterior.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Left main coronary artery</strong> → divides into:<ul>\n<li>the <strong>left anterior descending (LAD)</strong>, running down the front, which supplies the <strong>front of the left ventricle and most of the septum</strong>;</li>\n<li>the <strong>left circumflex (LCx)</strong>, curving round the left side, which supplies the <strong>side wall of the left ventricle</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Right coronary artery (RCA)</strong> supplies the <strong>right ventricle</strong> and, usually, the <strong>bottom (inferior) wall</strong> of the heart.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Dominance</strong> just means which artery gives off the <strong>posterior descending artery</strong>, which supplies the back of the septum. It is the RCA in most people (&quot;right dominant&quot;). It does <strong>not</strong> mean that artery supplies one whole side.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure dg-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 1. Major coronary routes and drainage — simplified flow map</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 366\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The aorta gives the left main and right coronary arteries. The left main divides into the LAD, supplying the front of the left ventricle and the septum, and the circumflex, supplying the side of the left ventricle. The right coronary supplies the right ventricle and inferior wall and, in most people, gives the posterior descending artery to the back of the septum (right dominant). Venous blood drains through the coronary sinus into the right atrium.\"><rect x=\"90\" y=\"10\" width=\"180\" height=\"40\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-red\"/><text x=\"180\" y=\"35\" class=\"dg-b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Aorta (aortic sinuses)</text><path d=\"M180 50V64H90V80M180 64H270V80\" class=\"dg-tree\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"80\" width=\"160\" height=\"40\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-red-soft\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"105\" class=\"dg-b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Left main</text><path d=\"M20 120V244H34M20 168H34\" class=\"dg-tree\"/><rect x=\"34\" y=\"140\" width=\"136\" height=\"56\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-red-soft\"/><text x=\"102\" y=\"163\" class=\"dg-b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">LAD</text><text x=\"102\" y=\"183\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Front of LV, septum</text><rect x=\"34\" y=\"216\" width=\"136\" height=\"56\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-red-soft\"/><text x=\"102\" y=\"239\" class=\"dg-b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Circumflex</text><text x=\"102\" y=\"259\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Side of LV</text><rect x=\"190\" y=\"80\" width=\"160\" height=\"72\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-red-soft\"/><text x=\"270\" y=\"103\" class=\"dg-b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Right coronary</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"123\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Right ventricle,</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"141\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\">inferior wall</text><path d=\"M200 152V200H214\" class=\"dg-tree\"/><rect x=\"214\" y=\"172\" width=\"136\" height=\"56\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-red-soft\"/><text x=\"282\" y=\"195\" class=\"dg-b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Posterior desc.</text><text x=\"282\" y=\"215\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Back of septum</text><text x=\"282\" y=\"250\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Usually from the RCA</text><text x=\"282\" y=\"268\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(right dominant)</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"300\" width=\"340\" height=\"56\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-blue\"/><text x=\"180\" y=\"323\" class=\"dg-b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Drainage</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"343\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Veins → coronary sinus → right atrium</text></svg><p>Conceptual branches, not an anatomical map of coronary vessel positions. Dominance and individual territories vary.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Drainage:</strong> most coronary venous blood collects in the <strong>coronary sinus</strong>, which empties into the <strong>right atrium</strong>. A little drains straight into the chambers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-is-left-ventricular-coronary-perfusion-greatest-during-diastole\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> Why is left ventricular coronary perfusion greatest during diastole?</h2>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure dg-figure\"><figcaption>Left coronary blood flow through one cardiac cycle — qualitative waveform</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 322\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Left coronary blood flow through one cardiac cycle. Flow drops to near zero early in systole, stays low through systole, rises steeply just after S2 to a peak in early diastole, then declines gradually.\"><rect x=\"62\" y=\"30\" width=\"108\" height=\"170\" class=\"dg-band-red\"/><rect x=\"170\" y=\"30\" width=\"176\" height=\"170\" class=\"dg-band-blue\"/><path d=\"M44 20V200H346\" class=\"dg-axis\"/><path d=\"M44 180H346\" class=\"dg-guide\"/><text x=\"116\" y=\"50\" class=\"dg-b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Systole</text><text x=\"258\" y=\"50\" class=\"dg-b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Diastole</text><path d=\"M46 132C54 130 58 132 62 140C70 176 78 186 92 182C116 176 140 170 166 168C176 168 178 120 186 84C190 66 200 66 212 72C250 92 290 118 344 130\" class=\"dg-curve-red\"/><text x=\"62\" y=\"218\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\">S1</text><text x=\"170\" y=\"218\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\">S2</text><text x=\"342\" y=\"218\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"end\">time →</text><text x=\"22\" y=\"112\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"rotate(-90 22 112)\">Left coronary flow</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"250\" class=\"dg-t\">Systole: contracting muscle squeezes the</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"268\" class=\"dg-t\">vessels inside it, so flow is low.</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"294\" class=\"dg-t\">Diastole: the muscle relaxes, the vessels</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"312\" class=\"dg-t\">reopen and flow peaks.</text></svg><p>Conceptual shape, not measured patient data: left-ventricular compression restricts flow during systole; relaxation allows a marked early-diastolic increase. Right coronary flow may be less strongly diastolic.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>In systole, the left ventricle squeezes its own arteries shut.</strong> The coronary branches run through the muscle wall. When the muscle contracts hard, it <strong>compresses</strong> them, and left ventricular coronary flow almost stops.</p>\n<p><strong>In diastole, the muscle relaxes and the arteries open.</strong> Aortic pressure is still high, so blood <strong>rushes in</strong>.</p>\n<p>So <strong>the left ventricle is fed mainly in diastole.</strong> The right ventricle, which squeezes far less hard, is fed in both <span data-term=\"systole-diastole\">systole and diastole</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>The pressure that drives left coronary flow</strong> ≈ <strong>aortic diastolic pressure − left ventricular diastolic pressure.</strong> Anything that lowers aortic diastolic pressure (shock, aortic regurgitation) or raises left ventricular filling pressure (heart failure) cuts this driving pressure.</p>\n<h3>Why is the inner layer (subendocardium) most at risk?</h3>\n<p>The <strong>inner layer</strong> of the left ventricle is squeezed hardest, because it is closest to the high-pressure cavity. So it depends <strong>most</strong> on diastole, and it is the <strong>first to become ischaemic</strong> when perfusion falls. In <strong>aortic stenosis</strong>, for example, very high ventricular pressure both raises oxygen demand and squeezes the inner-layer vessels harder.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 1–3\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–3 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why must coronary flow rise when the heart works harder?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The heart already extracts most of the oxygen from its blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The coronary arteries narrow during exercise</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Blood carries more oxygen during exercise</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The heart can store extra oxygen for later use</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"coronary:560b661c\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> With about 70% extraction at rest, there is little more to take, so more oxygen needs more flow.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why does the left ventricle receive most of its coronary flow in diastole?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Aortic pressure is highest in diastole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Its contracting muscle squeezes its own arteries in systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The coronary arteries close their valves in systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The right ventricle takes all the flow during systole</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"coronary:ecbfdb23\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Contraction compresses the arteries running through the wall. When the muscle relaxes, they open and fill.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Which part of the left ventricle becomes ischaemic first when perfusion falls?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The outer layer, nearest the surface arteries</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The inner layer (subendocardium), squeezed hardest in systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The papillary muscles only</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. All layers equally at the same time</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"coronary:7ecd1f61\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The inner layer is compressed most and depends most on diastolic flow.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"4-how-does-coronary-flow-match-changing-myocardial-work\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> How does coronary flow match changing myocardial work?</h2>\n<p>The heart's oxygen demand rises with <strong>heart rate</strong>, <strong>contractility</strong> and <strong><span data-term=\"wall-tension\">wall tension</span></strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/co-measurement?section=7-why-does-pressure-loading-increase-the-heart-s-oxygen-demand\">→ Measuring Cardiac Output, §7</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Working heart muscle opens its own arteries.</strong> As it works harder, it releases local signals, chiefly <strong>adenosine</strong> and also K⁺, low O₂ and <strong><span data-term=\"nitric-oxide\">nitric oxide</span></strong> from the endothelium. These relax the coronary arterioles, so flow rises to match the work. This is <strong>active hyperaemia</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/local-regulation\">→ Local Control</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sympathetic nerves: two effects, one winner.</strong> They can constrict coronary arteries directly (α receptors). But they also make the heart beat faster and harder (β₁), which raises its metabolism and releases dilating metabolites. <strong>The dilation wins</strong>, so in exercise coronary flow rises.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-can-tachycardia-cause-ischaemia\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> Why can tachycardia cause ischaemia?</h2>\n<p>A fast heart hits the heart muscle from <strong>both</strong> sides:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Demand rises:</strong> more beats per minute means more work.</li>\n<li><strong>Supply time falls:</strong> as heart rate rises, <strong>diastole shortens much more than systole</strong>. Diastole is when the left ventricle is perfused.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"diastole-clock\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Diastole clock</figcaption><p>A heart-rate slider (60–180 beats/min). One beat is drawn as a bar split into systole and diastole, to scale. Below it: one minute's worth of diastole, in seconds. Readout: \"At 70: 0.86 s per beat, diastole ≈ 0.57 s. At 150: 0.40 s per beat, diastole ≈ 0.16 s.\" A line warns when diastole falls below about a third of the beat: \"the left ventricle has little time to be perfused\".</p></figure>\n\n<p>In a healthy heart, the arterioles dilate enough to cope. But with a <strong>narrowed coronary artery</strong>, a <strong>low diastolic pressure</strong> or a <strong>high filling pressure</strong>, the shrinking diastole can tip the balance into <strong>ischaemia</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-coronary-flow-reserve-angina-and-myocardial-infarction\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.</span> Coronary flow reserve, angina and myocardial infarction</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Coronary_Artery_Disease.png?width=800\" srcset=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Coronary_Artery_Disease.png?width=480 480w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Coronary_Artery_Disease.png?width=800 800w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Coronary_Artery_Disease.png?width=1200 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 700px) 92vw, 760px\" alt=\"Medical comparison of normal coronary artery flow and an artery narrowed by atherosclerotic plaque.\"><figcaption><strong>Normal coronary artery versus a narrowed vessel.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">BruceBlaus, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coronary_Artery_Disease.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Coronary flow reserve</strong> is how much coronary flow can rise above its resting level when the arterioles fully dilate. Normally, it can rise about 4–5 times.</p>\n<p><strong>Why angina comes on with exertion, not at rest:</strong> behind a narrowed artery, the arterioles <strong>dilate at rest</strong> to keep flow normal. They have <strong>used up their reserve</strong>. When exercise raises demand, there is nothing left to give, so supply falls short and <strong>angina</strong> (ischaemic chest pain) appears. Resting cures it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Angina</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Myocardial infarction (heart attack)</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>What happens</td>\n  <td>Temporary <strong>ischaemia</strong>: supply falls short of demand</td>\n  <td>Prolonged, severe ischaemia: <strong>heart muscle dies</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Reversible?</td>\n  <td><strong>Yes</strong>, when demand falls or flow returns</td>\n  <td><strong>No</strong>: dead muscle becomes scar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Usual cause</td>\n  <td>A fixed narrowing plus higher demand</td>\n  <td>A plaque ruptures and a clot blocks the artery</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 4–6\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 4–6 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> What mainly dilates the coronary arterioles when the heart works harder?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Parasympathetic nerves releasing acetylcholine</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Local signals such as adenosine from working heart muscle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Direct sympathetic stimulation of α receptors</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. A rise in coronary venous pressure</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"coronary:7ccefe7b\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Metabolic signals from the working muscle (adenosine, K⁺, low O₂, nitric oxide) relax the arterioles: active hyperaemia.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why can a heart rate of 160 cause ischaemia in a patient with coronary narrowing?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It raises oxygen demand and shortens diastole, when the left ventricle is fed</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It raises aortic diastolic pressure too high</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It makes the coronary arteries contract more strongly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It increases the time the left ventricle is perfused</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"coronary:4e676da0\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Demand rises while the time available for left ventricular perfusion shrinks.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A man has chest pain on climbing stairs that goes when he rests. What is the best explanation?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. A coronary artery is completely blocked, killing heart muscle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A narrowed artery has used up its reserve, so supply can't rise with demand</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. His heart muscle is extracting too little oxygen</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. His coronary veins are blocked during exercise</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"coronary:8c41079c\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The arterioles behind the narrowing are already dilated at rest. On exertion, supply can't rise, which gives stable angina.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"7-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Coronary arteries</strong> arise from the aortic sinuses. The heart extracts about 70% of its oxygen at rest, so more demand needs <strong>more flow</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>LAD</strong> = front of the LV and the septum; <strong>circumflex</strong> = side of the LV; <strong>RCA</strong> = right ventricle and inferior wall. <strong>Dominance</strong> = which artery gives the posterior descending artery.</li>\n<li><strong>Venous blood</strong> drains via the <strong>coronary sinus</strong> to the right atrium.</li>\n<li><strong>The left ventricle is fed mainly in diastole</strong>, because contraction squeezes its arteries. Driving pressure ≈ aortic diastolic − LV diastolic pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>The <span data-term=\"subendocardium\">subendocardium</span></strong> is the most vulnerable layer: it is squeezed hardest in systole, so it depends most on diastole and becomes ischaemic first.</li>\n<li><strong>Active hyperaemia:</strong> adenosine and other metabolites dilate the arterioles. Sympathetic β₁ stimulation makes the heart work harder, so the extra metabolites outweigh the direct α constriction and the net result is dilation.</li>\n<li><strong>Tachycardia</strong> raises demand and <strong>shortens diastole</strong>, so it can cause ischaemia in diseased arteries.</li>\n<li><strong>Narrowed artery</strong> → reserve used up at rest → <strong>angina on exertion</strong>. <strong>Infarction</strong> = muscle death from prolonged ischaemia.</li>\n</ol>\n";
